75, -0.031494140625, -0.0245361328125, -0.006168365478515625, -0.007076263427734375, 0.004730224609375, 0.0271453857421875, -0.002655029296875, 0.0037174224853515625, 0.0114288330078125, -0.0210723876953125, -0.009979248046875, 0.03485107421875, 0.0144195556640625, 0.035003662109375, -0.0031490325927734375, 0.01483154296875, -0.0167236328125, 0.03564453125, 0.0119781494140625, 0.003955841064453125, 0.00818634033203125, -0.022857666015625, -0.00917816162109375, 0.01407623291015625, -0.0009317398071289062, -0.0202178955078125, 0.0338134765625, -0.0008034706115722656, 0.0231781005859375, -0.00926971435546875, -0.003345489501953125, -0.016021728515625, 0.006351470947265625, 0.0220947265625, -0.01445770263671875, 0.014251708984375, -0.006465911865234375, -0.01352691650390625, 0.01580810546875, -0.0361328125, -0.017181396484375, 0.00966644287109375, -0.0079345703125, 0.01215362548828125, -0.002017974853515625, 0.03411865234375, 0.03546142578125, -0.010345458984375, -0.007904052734375, -0.0020160675048828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance in an exploratory single-institution pilot study.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective, exploratory study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024 from a single academic institution. No external datasets were used for model development or validation. The sample size was determined by reviewer workload feasibility and the need for representation across flag categories, reflecting the pilot nature of the study; as such, effect size estimation and generalizability are limited.\n\nMulti-Agent AI System Architecture:\n\n1. **Rule-Based Section Labeling Agent**\n   - **Input:** Report sentences.\n   - **Operation:** Applied deterministic regular expression (regex) patterns\u2014constructed from curated anatomical term lists\u2014to assign each sentence to an anatomical section (e.g., \u201clungs,\u201d \u201cliver\u201d). Overlapping terms were resolved using explicit precedence hierarchy rules (e.g., \u201cliver\u201d > \u201ckidney\u201d if both present). Term lists were compiled from consensus radiology lexicons, refined iteratively based on in-house report review. Example: The sentence \u201cThere is a hypodense lesion in the right kidney and left liver lobe\u201d would be labeled \u201cliver\u201d per predetermined hierarchy.\n   - **Output:** Sentences with anatomical section tags.\n\n2. **Locally Deployed Language Model Agent**\n   - **Input:** Anatomically segmented report text output from the first agent.\n   - **Operation:** Llama 2 13B (locally hosted) was further fine-tuned on 15,000 de-identified in-house radiology reports, non-overlapping with the evaluation set (learning rate 2e-5, batch size 8, three epochs, 10% validation split). Llama 2 13B was selected due to its balance of strong accuracy, computational efficiency, and deployment feasibility compared to smaller (with lower capacity) or much larger (less tractable locally) alternatives (see Touvron et al., 2023; benchmarking in Supplementary Table 1). Fine-tuning prioritized lowest validation loss and highest per-label F1 while avoiding overfitting, as shown by stable performance on held-out internal data. No external data or ML-based probability thresholds were utilized; output consisted of section-assigned report sentences.\n   - **Output:** Structured, labeled reports.\n\n3. **Deterministic QA Agent**\n   - **Input:** Structured reports plus patient demographic metadata.\n   - **Operation:** Applied deterministic, rule-based logic:\n     - **Section mismatch:** Flagged abnormality sentences present in Findings but absent from Impression (and vice versa) via keyword co-occurrence and negation detection.\n     - **Gender-anatomy conflict:** Cross-referenced demographic metadata and text (e.g., mention of \u201cuterus\u201d for male patients).\n     - **Undocumented communication:** Flagged if \u201ccritical finding,\u201d \u201curgent,\u201d or similar terms were present with no explicit provider notification documentation.\n   - **Output:** Binary QA flags for each report. See Supplementary Material S1 for representative rules and anatomical lists.\n\nEvaluation:\n\nA stratified random subset of 45 reports was selected for manual review, sampling 20 section mismatch, 10 gender-anatomy conflict, 10 undocumented communication, and 5 unflagged cases. Two board-certified radiologists independently evaluated each output using four operationalized metrics:\n   - **Fidelity:** All information/meaning of original report preserved; judged per sentence.\n   - **Accuracy:** Correct section/abnormality assignment; per sentence.\n   - **Omission:** Any clinically important content absent.\n   - **Fabrication:** Any statement not present or justifiably inferred from the report.\n\nReviewers worked independently and were blinded to QA status and patient metadata. Each reviewer recorded categorical ratings for each metric (see Supplementary Table S2 for explicit guidance). Ambiguous or discordant ratings prompted joint discussion; consensus was defined as identical ratings on all four metrics. Five cases without consensus were adjudicated by a third board-certified radiologist. The review process and decision algorithm are visually summarized in Figure 1. Ratings for overall QA performance were: \u201cexcellent\u201d (no errors, high clarity), \u201cgood\u201d (minor, non-clinically significant errors), \u201cfair\u201d (structural deficiencies without critical omission/fabrication).\n\nStatistical Analysis:\n\nGiven the limited, non-powered pilot sample, only descriptive statistics and proportions are reported. Inter-reviewer reliability was estimated via unweighted and weighted Cohen\u2019s kappa. Confidence intervals for proportions (e.g., error-free rates) are interpreted with caution due to stratified, non-simple random sampling.\n\nResults:\n\nThe multi-agent system restructured Findings sections from all reports (22,270 sentences) into predefined anatomical formats, retaining original content. The system flagged 90/638 (14.1%) reports: 80 (12.5%) section mismatches, 5 (0.8%) gender-anatomy conflicts, and 5 (0.8%) undocumented communication, per rule-based criteria. Examples (Table 1):\n   - Section mismatch: \u201cRenal mass\u201d in Findings, absent from Impression.\n   - Gender-anatomy conflict: \u201cProstate\u201d referenced in report for female patient.\n   - Undocumented communication: \u201cUrgent pulmonary embolism\u201d with no notification statement.\n\nIn the 45-report subset:\n   - 31 reports (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were rated correctly restructured.\n   - 2 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201312%) were incorrectly structured (most common errors: mis-assignment of multi-organ findings, e.g., \u201cliver and kidney lesions\u201d misattributed due to hierarchy rules).\n   - 12 reports (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%) had initial reviewer disagreement (frequently over \u201cfair\u201d vs. \u201cgood\u201d assignments in borderline section-mapping cases).\n   - Cohen\u2019s kappa for binary correct/incorrect structuring was 0.49 (moderate agreement); weighted kappa for three-category QA ratings was 0.55.\n   - Consensus reached after discussion in 40/45 cases; 5 adjudicated.\n   - No cases had clinically important omissions or fabricated content (0%, CI bounds overlap zero).\n   - Final QA ratings: \u201cExcellent\u201d 17 (38%), \u201cGood\u201d 21 (46%), \u201cFair\u201d 7 (16%). Common structuring errors are summarized in Table 2 (see Supplementary Material S3 for details).\n\nDiscussion:\n\nThis pilot is limited by single-institution dataset, lack of external validation, and modest, stratified sample size, limiting precision in effect estimates and generalizability. Stratification may introduce spectrum bias, as the proportions of flagged error types are not representative of broader clinical distributions, while reviewer preconceptions may contribute to calibration drift. Fine-tuning the Llama 2 13B model solely on internal reports may lead to overfitting to local language idiosyncrasies, potentially inflating apparent accuracy. Rule-based term lists and hierarchical logic, though reproducible, may miss edge cases not captured in explicit patterns; see Supplement for representative rules/lists and construction workflow. Reviewer disagreements often arose from challenging compound findings or ambiguous sentence boundaries, highlighting both the complexity of natural report language and inherent subjectivity in post-hoc structuring.\n\nRecommendations for future work include expanding reviewer blinding, using larger and more diverse multi-institutional report samples, benchmarking against alternative structuring or QA approaches, and assessing the robustness of agent rules/models to unencountered formats or colloquialisms.\n\nConclusion:\n\nA locally deployed multi-agent AI system integrated deterministic rule sets and a fine-tuned local language model for radiology report structuring and quality assurance. In this exploratory pilot review of 45 stratified reports, the system demonstrated favorable structuring performance\u2014majority \u201cexcellent\u201d/\u201cgood\u201d ratings, no clinically significant errors, and moderate reviewer agreement. However, findings are limited by sample size, potential for spectrum and reviewer bias, and single-site development and validation. Broader evaluation is essential to establish generalizability, robustness, and clinical utility for radiology quality assurance workflows.\n\n(Supplementary Materials referenced are not included here but are available upon request.)"}}